1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,691
There is a town in Pennsylvania where the ground is hot.

2
00:00:03,911 --> 00:00:06,062
Where smoke comes out of cracks in the road.

3
00:00:06,282 --> 00:00:10,023
Where the streets are still there, but almost all the houses are gone.

4
00:00:10,523 --> 00:00:14,450
Underneath it, a fire has been burning since nineteen sixty two.

5
00:00:14,950 --> 00:00:16,586
It's still burning right now.

6
00:00:16,806 --> 00:00:17,915
While you watch this.

7
00:00:18,135 --> 00:00:21,074
It might still be burning when your grandkids watch this.

8
00:00:22,474 --> 00:00:27,221
Hi, I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and today's file is Centralia.

9
00:00:27,441 --> 00:00:30,664
The real town that helped inspire the Silent Hill movie.

10
00:00:31,164 --> 00:00:36,508
A town that lost its people, its houses, and eventually even its ZIP code.

11
00:00:36,727 --> 00:00:39,302
Because of one fire that nobody could put out.

12
00:00:39,802 --> 00:00:44,445
Let's find out how it started, why it never stopped, and what's left there today.

13
00:00:45,845 --> 00:00:47,978
First, Centralia before the fire.

14
00:00:48,198 --> 00:00:58,292
It was a small coal mining town in eastern Pennsylvania, with churches, shops, bars, and around a thousand people by the late nineteen seventies.

15
00:00:58,792 --> 00:01:00,323
And under the town was coal.

16
00:01:00,543 --> 00:01:01,322
Lots of it.

17
00:01:01,542 --> 00:01:04,803
Miners had dug tunnels through it for about a hundred years.

18
00:01:05,303 --> 00:01:09,052
By the nineteen sixties, many of those mines were abandoned.

19
00:01:09,272 --> 00:01:15,376
Which left a giant network of empty tunnels, full of coal, running right under people's homes.

20
00:01:15,876 --> 00:01:18,069
You can probably see where this is going.

21
00:01:18,289 --> 00:01:19,365
The town couldn't.

22
00:01:20,765 --> 00:01:26,372
In May nineteen sixty two, the town needed to clean up its landfill before Memorial Day.

23
00:01:26,592 --> 00:01:30,276
The landfill was an old strip mining pit at the edge of town.

24
00:01:30,776 --> 00:01:37,667
The most common account says volunteer firefighters set the trash on fire to burn it down, then put it out.

25
00:01:38,167 --> 00:01:39,341
Or thought they did.

26
00:01:39,841 --> 00:01:45,503
The fire found a hole in the pit wall, and crept into the old coal mine tunnels underneath.

27
00:01:46,003 --> 00:01:47,068
Spring cleaning.

28
00:01:47,288 --> 00:01:50,773
Highly not recommended with a coal mine in the basement.

29
00:01:52,173 --> 00:01:54,929
Now, here's why a coal mine fire is a nightmare.

30
00:01:55,149 --> 00:01:56,843
It is not like a house fire.

31
00:01:57,343 --> 00:02:01,729
Underground, the fire can creep slowly along the coal seams for miles.

32
00:02:01,949 --> 00:02:03,300
It doesn't need much air.

33
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:06,451
It finds it through cracks, old shafts and holes.

34
00:02:06,951 --> 00:02:08,628
Officials tried to dig it out.

35
00:02:08,848 --> 00:02:09,991
They tried to flood it.

36
00:02:10,211 --> 00:02:11,765
They tried to fill the tunnels.

37
00:02:11,985 --> 00:02:15,733
Every attempt was too small, too slow, or ran out of money.

38
00:02:16,233 --> 00:02:24,795
Fighting an underground coal fire with a few trucks of dirt is like trying to stop a forest fire by politely asking it to leave.

39
00:02:26,195 --> 00:02:28,216
For years, life mostly went on.

40
00:02:28,436 --> 00:02:35,302
Then, in nineteen seventy nine, the mayor, who owned a gas station, checked the fuel in his underground tank.

41
00:02:35,802 --> 00:02:38,760
It was a hundred and seventy two degrees Fahrenheit.

42
00:02:38,980 --> 00:02:40,853
That's about seventy eight Celsius.

43
00:02:41,073 --> 00:02:41,900
Gasoline.

44
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:43,020
Underground.

45
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:44,230
Nearly cooking.

46
00:02:44,730 --> 00:02:46,628
That's not a gas station anymore.

47
00:02:46,848 --> 00:02:48,917
That's a very expensive slow cooker.

48
00:02:50,317 --> 00:02:53,724
Then came the moment that made the whole country pay attention.

49
00:02:54,224 --> 00:03:03,252
In nineteen eighty one, a twelve year old boy named Todd Domboski was in his grandmother's backyard when the ground opened under his feet.

50
00:03:03,752 --> 00:03:11,937
He fell into a hole about a hundred and fifty feet deep, full of hot, poisonous steam, and managed to grab onto tree roots.

51
00:03:12,437 --> 00:03:13,829
His cousin pulled him out.

52
00:03:14,049 --> 00:03:14,995
He survived.

53
00:03:15,495 --> 00:03:21,130
When they tested the steam coming out of that hole, it had a deadly level of carbon monoxide.

54
00:03:21,350 --> 00:03:23,146
And that's not even the scariest part.

55
00:03:23,366 --> 00:03:27,411
It was a normal backyard, in a normal neighbourhood, on a normal day.

56
00:03:28,811 --> 00:03:31,144
After that, the government finally acted.

57
00:03:31,364 --> 00:03:32,821
Not to put out the fire.

58
00:03:33,041 --> 00:03:34,140
To move the people.

59
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:43,866
In nineteen eighty three, Congress set aside more than forty two million dollars to buy the homes in Centralia and relocate the residents.

60
00:03:44,366 --> 00:03:45,897
Most people took the deal.

61
00:03:46,117 --> 00:03:48,045
More than a thousand people left.

62
00:03:48,265 --> 00:03:50,824
Around five hundred buildings were torn down.

63
00:03:51,324 --> 00:03:58,003
Imagine your entire hometown being sold, house by house, until there's nothing left but sidewalks.

64
00:03:59,403 --> 00:04:01,337
But some people refused to leave.

65
00:04:01,837 --> 00:04:07,388
In nineteen ninety two, the state used eminent domain to claim every property in the town.

66
00:04:07,608 --> 00:04:09,933
The holdouts fought it in court, and lost.

67
00:04:10,433 --> 00:04:16,017
Then, in two thousand two, the Postal Service took away Centralia's ZIP code.

68
00:04:16,237 --> 00:04:17,870
One seven nine two seven.

69
00:04:18,090 --> 00:04:18,663
Gone.

70
00:04:19,163 --> 00:04:23,260
You know a town is in trouble when even the mail stops believing in it.

71
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:27,623
In two thousand thirteen, the last residents reached a settlement.

72
00:04:27,843 --> 00:04:30,641
They could stay in their homes for the rest of their lives.

73
00:04:30,861 --> 00:04:32,851
After that, the state takes the land.

74
00:04:33,071 --> 00:04:36,199
By the twenty twenty census, five people lived there.

75
00:04:37,599 --> 00:04:38,874
And then there's the road.

76
00:04:39,094 --> 00:04:45,954
A stretch of Route sixty one cracked and buckled from the heat underneath, so the state rerouted traffic around it.

77
00:04:46,454 --> 00:04:50,097
Visitors started covering the abandoned road in spray paint.

78
00:04:50,317 --> 00:04:51,095
Years of it.

79
00:04:51,315 --> 00:04:55,130
It became an internet famous spot called the Graffiti Highway.

80
00:04:55,630 --> 00:05:00,529
Until twenty twenty, when the owners buried the whole thing under piles of dirt.

81
00:05:00,749 --> 00:05:05,429
Possibly the only time in history that art was cancelled with a bulldozer.

82
00:05:06,829 --> 00:05:08,443
So is the fire still burning?

83
00:05:08,663 --> 00:05:09,248
Yes.

84
00:05:09,748 --> 00:05:11,813
It has spread under hundreds of acres.

85
00:05:12,033 --> 00:05:17,914
A two thousand six report estimated it could keep burning for another two hundred and fifty years.

86
00:05:18,414 --> 00:05:23,049
Today, Centralia mostly looks like a quiet field with streets running through it.

87
00:05:23,269 --> 00:05:24,274
A few houses.

88
00:05:24,494 --> 00:05:25,720
A church on the hill.

89
00:05:25,940 --> 00:05:30,078
Warning signs about unstable ground and dangerous gases.

90
00:05:30,578 --> 00:05:36,324
In some places, especially in cold weather, you can still see steam coming out of the ground.

91
00:05:37,724 --> 00:05:39,441
Now, the Silent Hill connection.

92
00:05:39,661 --> 00:05:41,464
Is the game set in Centralia?

93
00:05:41,964 --> 00:05:42,474
No.

94
00:05:42,694 --> 00:05:46,038
The original games were not based on any real town.

95
00:05:46,258 --> 00:05:52,391
But the screenwriter of the two thousand six Silent Hill movie researched Centralia while writing it.

96
00:05:52,611 --> 00:05:55,721
A town with fog, ash, and fire under the streets.

97
00:05:55,941 --> 00:05:57,104
It fit perfectly.

98
00:05:57,604 --> 00:05:59,530
So the real place is not cursed.

99
00:05:59,750 --> 00:06:01,055
It's just on fire.

100
00:06:01,275 --> 00:06:04,245
Which, honestly, is worse for property values.

101
00:06:05,645 --> 00:06:07,370
So here's the honest version.

102
00:06:07,870 --> 00:06:08,808
Documented.

103
00:06:09,028 --> 00:06:13,318
A fire started in the town's coal mines around nineteen sixty two.

104
00:06:13,538 --> 00:06:15,041
Efforts to stop it failed.

105
00:06:15,261 --> 00:06:21,927
After a boy nearly died in a sinkhole in nineteen eighty one, the government paid to relocate the town.

106
00:06:22,147 --> 00:06:25,371
Its ZIP code was revoked in two thousand two.

107
00:06:25,591 --> 00:06:26,976
The fire still burns.

108
00:06:27,476 --> 00:06:28,334
Disputed.

109
00:06:28,554 --> 00:06:30,043
Exactly how it started.

110
00:06:30,263 --> 00:06:39,102
The landfill burn is the most common account, but there are other theories, including hot ash dumped in the pit, or an older mine fire.

111
00:06:39,602 --> 00:06:40,332
Legend.

112
00:06:40,552 --> 00:06:45,865
That Centralia is a cursed ghost town, or that the Silent Hill games were based on it.

113
00:06:47,265 --> 00:06:48,251
So, your turn.

114
00:06:48,471 --> 00:06:54,014
If the government offered to buy your house because of a fire under your street, would you leave?

115
00:06:54,234 --> 00:06:56,465
Or would you be one of the people who stayed?

116
00:06:56,965 --> 00:06:58,209
Tell me in the comments.

117
00:06:58,429 --> 00:06:59,218
I'd leave.

118
00:06:59,438 --> 00:07:00,573
I'm made of paper.

119
00:07:00,793 --> 00:07:02,608
This is not my kind of town.

120
00:07:03,108 --> 00:07:07,187
If you like true stories that history never quite finished, subscribe.

121
00:07:07,407 --> 00:07:11,789
I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and I'll see you in the next file.
